IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `ssc-core`
IMPORT `ssc6-smp`

-- Social Security Contributions and Benefits Act 1992 (United Kingdom), 1992 c. 4.
-- LAYER 1, part ssc6: Part XI, Statutory Sick Pay (ss 151-163).
-- Lines 3095-3240 of ukpga-1992-4.txt, a plain-text rendering made by a converter script.
-- It is not the official text, and nothing here is "verified against the official text".
-- It imports ssc6-smp because s 153(12) sends "confinement", "maternity pay period" and the
-- other terms of the disqualifying period to ss 171(1) and 165(1), which that module encodes.
--
-- How to read a result (the rule of 2026-10-29, assumed and not ruled by a lawyer):
--   JUST FALSE answers "the text says no in its own words" and carries a comment
--       -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "<the quoted words>"
--   NOTHING answers "not covered" and carries a comment  -- NOTHING-kind (<why>)
--   A caller must never turn NOTHING into FALSE.
-- Money is a NUMBER in pounds sterling; a figure is weekly unless its name says otherwise.
-- The figures the Act states in its own words are constants here (s 157(1): £123.25 and 80 per
-- cent; s 155(4): 28 times; s 152(3): 8 weeks). An order under s 157(2), or regulations under
-- s 152(4), could substitute others, and would change the answers.

§ `Part XI -- Statutory Sick Pay`

§§ `s 151 Employer's liability`

-- Helper (it implements no provision): the three-valued AND of a list of answers.
-- JUST FALSE if any answer is JUST FALSE; JUST TRUE if every answer is JUST TRUE; NOTHING otherwise.
-- The JUST FALSE it returns is the "no" of the calling rule, whose own comment quotes the text.
GIVEN answers IS A LIST OF MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 151(1) all of these answers hold` MEANS
    CONSIDER answers
    WHEN EMPTY THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN first FOLLOWED BY rest THEN
        CONSIDER first
        WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
        WHEN JUST TRUE THEN `s 151(1) all of these answers hold` rest
        WHEN NOTHING THEN
            CONSIDER `s 151(1) all of these answers hold` rest
            WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
            WHEN JUST TRUE THEN NOTHING
            WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

-- Plain gloss: a "day of incapacity for work" is a day on which the employee is, or regulations
-- deem him to be, unable by reason of some specific disease or bodily or mental disablement to do
-- work he could reasonably be expected to do under that contract.
-- src:3105 | (4) For the purposes of this Part of this Act a day of incapacity for work in relation to a contract of service means a day on which the employee concerned is, or is deemed in accordance with regulations to be, incapable by reason of some specific disease or bodily or mental disablement of doing work which he can reasonably be expected to do under that contract.
@ref s 151(4) (src:3105)
GIVEN `the employee is incapable, by reason of some specific disease or bodily or mental disablement, of doing work which he can reasonably be expected to do under that contract` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the employee is deemed in accordance with regulations to be so incapable` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 151(4) the day is a day of incapacity for work in relation to the contract of service` MEANS
    IF     `the employee is incapable, by reason of some specific disease or bodily or mental disablement, of doing work which he can reasonably be expected to do under that contract`
        OR `the employee is deemed in accordance with regulations to be so incapable`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "a day of incapacity for work in relation to a contract of service means a day on which the employee concerned is, or is deemed in accordance with regulations to be, incapable"

-- Plain gloss: the employer has to pay statutory sick pay for a day on which the employee has a
-- day of incapacity for work, if the first condition (the day is in a period of entitlement,
-- s 153) and the second (the day is a qualifying day, s 154) are met. The payment is "in
-- accordance with the following provisions", so the rate, the limit and the bars still apply.
-- src:3098 | (1) Where an employee has a day of incapacity for work in relation to his contract of service with an employer, that employer shall, if the conditions set out in sections 153 and 154 below are satisfied, be liable to make him, in accordance with the following provisions of this Part of this Act, a payment (to be known as “statutory sick pay”) in respect of that day.
@ref s 151(1) (src:3098)
GIVEN `the day is a day of incapacity for work in relation to the contract of service` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the first condition in section 153 is satisfied` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the second condition in section 154 is satisfied` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 151(1) the employer is liable to make a payment of statutory sick pay in respect of the day` MEANS
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "that employer shall, if the conditions set out in sections 153 and 154 below are satisfied, be liable" (marginal reading (a): a failed "if" counts as the text's no)
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): a condition that depends on regulations or on Schedule 11 is NOTHING when the caller does not supply it
    `s 151(1) all of these answers hold` (LIST `the day is a day of incapacity for work in relation to the contract of service`, `the first condition in section 153 is satisfied`, `the second condition in section 154 is satisfied`)

-- Plain gloss: a contract term cannot take away or cut down statutory sick pay, or make an
-- employee pay towards the employer's costs of it. "To the extent that it purports" limits the
-- voidness to what the agreement tries to do.
-- src:3099 | (2) Any agreement shall be void to the extent that it purports—
-- src:3100 | (a) to exclude, limit or otherwise modify any provision of this Part of this Act, or
-- src:3101 | (b) to require an employee to contribute (whether directly or indirectly) towards any costs incurred by his employer under this Part of this Act.
@ref s 151(2) (src:3099-3101)
GIVEN `the agreement purports to exclude, limit or otherwise modify a provision of Part XI` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the agreement purports to require an employee to contribute, directly or indirectly, towards costs incurred by his employer under Part XI` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 151(2) the agreement is void to the extent that it purports` MEANS
    IF     `the agreement purports to exclude, limit or otherwise modify a provision of Part XI`
        OR `the agreement purports to require an employee to contribute, directly or indirectly, towards costs incurred by his employer under Part XI`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "to the extent that it purports"

-- Plain gloss: the safe harbour. An agreement that lets the employer deduct from statutory sick
-- pay is not void under (2)(a) if the employer could make the same deductions from contractual
-- pay for the same period, or could if it were paying contractual pay.
-- src:3102 | (3) For the avoidance of doubt, any agreement between an employer and an employee authorising any deductions from statutory sick pay which the employer is liable to pay to the employee in respect of any period shall not be void by virtue of subsection (2)(a) above if the employer—
-- src:3103 | (a) is authorised by that or another agreement to make the same deductions from any contractual remuneration which he is liable to pay in respect of the same period, or
-- src:3104 | (b) would be so authorised if he were liable to pay contractual remuneration in respect of that period.
@ref s 151(3) (src:3102-3104)
GIVEN `the agreement authorises deductions from statutory sick pay that the employer is liable to pay in respect of a period` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the employer is authorised by that or another agreement to make the same deductions from contractual remuneration he is liable to pay in respect of the same period` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the employer would be so authorised if he were liable to pay contractual remuneration in respect of that period` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 151(3) the agreement is protected from being void by virtue of subsection (2)(a)` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the agreement authorises deductions from statutory sick pay that the employer is liable to pay in respect of a period`
            THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the subsection speaks only of "any agreement ... authorising any deductions"
        IF    `the employer is authorised by that or another agreement to make the same deductions from contractual remuneration he is liable to pay in respect of the same period`
           OR `the employer would be so authorised if he were liable to pay contractual remuneration in respect of that period`
            THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "shall not be void by virtue of subsection (2)(a) above if the employer" (marginal reading (a): a failed "if" counts as the text's no)

-- Plain gloss: if an employee has two or more contracts with the same employer, the Part treats
-- the employer as a different employer for each contract, except in prescribed cases.
-- src:3106 | (5) In any case where an employee has more than one contract of service with the same employer the provisions of this Part of this Act shall, except in such cases as may be prescribed and subject to the following provisions of this Part of this Act, have effect as if the employer were a different employer in relation to each contract of service.
@ref s 151(5) (src:3106)
GIVEN `the employee has more than one contract of service with the same employer` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the case is one prescribed under subsection (5)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 151(5) the employer is treated as a different employer in relation to each contract of service` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the case is one prescribed under subsection (5)`
            THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "except in such cases as may be prescribed"
        IF `the employee has more than one contract of service with the same employer`
            THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the subsection speaks only of an employee with more than one contract

-- Plain gloss: regulations can make the Commissioners of Inland Revenue, not the employer, liable
-- to pay in prescribed circumstances. The power is machinery; what it means to the employee is
-- who pays. Whether the circumstances are prescribed is a fact outside the sources.
DECLARE `s 151 Payer` IS ONE OF
    `s 151 the employer`
    `s 151 the Commissioners of Inland Revenue`

-- src:3107 | (6) Circumstances may be prescribed in which, notwithstanding the provisions of subsections (1) to (5) above, the liability to make payments of statutory sick pay is to be a liability of the Commissioners of Inland Revenue.
@ref s 151(6) (src:3107)
GIVEN `the circumstances are prescribed under subsection (6)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE `s 151 Payer`
`s 151(6) who is liable to make payments of statutory sick pay` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the circumstances are prescribed under subsection (6)`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): which circumstances are prescribed is for regulations
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST `s 151 the Commissioners of Inland Revenue`
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST `s 151 the employer`        -- subsection (1) puts the liability on "that employer"

§§ `s 152 Period of incapacity for work`

-- The Act's own figure in subsection (3). An order under subsection (4) may substitute a larger number.
-- src:3113 | (3) Any two periods of incapacity for work which are separated by a period of not more than 8 weeks shall be treated as a single period of incapacity for work.
@ref s 152(3) (src:3113)
`s 152(3) the number of weeks specified in the Act` MEANS 8

-- Plain gloss: a "period of incapacity for work" is one day, or two or more consecutive days,
-- each a day of incapacity for work. Subsection (5) says every day of the week counts, so the
-- caller counts calendar days (Sundays and weekends included).
-- src:3112 | (2) In this Part of this Act “period of incapacity for work” means a period of one day which is, or of two or more consecutive days each of which is, a day of incapacity for work in relation to the contract of service in question.
@ref s 152(2) (src:3112)
GIVEN `the number of days in the stretch, counting every day of the week` IS A NUMBER
      `the number of those days that are days of incapacity for work` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 152(2) the days form a period of incapacity for work` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the number of days in the stretch, counting every day of the week` AT MOST 0
            THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): a stretch of no days is neither one day nor two or more
        IF `the number of those days that are days of incapacity for work` EQUALS `the number of days in the stretch, counting every day of the week`
            THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“period of incapacity for work” means a period of one day which is, or of two or more consecutive days each of which is, a day of incapacity for work"

-- Plain gloss: the number of days from the first to the last day of a period, both included, counting every day of the week.
-- src:3115 | (5) No day of the week shall be disregarded in calculating any period of consecutive days for the purposes of this section.
@ref s 152(5) (src:3115)
GIVEN `the first day` IS A DATE
      `the last day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 152(5) the number of days in the period counting every day of the week` MEANS
    (`the last day` MINUS `the first day`) PLUS 1

-- Plain gloss: two periods of incapacity for work separated by not more than 8 weeks (a week
-- is any 7 days, s 163(1)) are treated as one. The days "between" are the days strictly between
-- the last day of the earlier period and the first day of the later one (fork F-gap).
-- src:3113 | (3) Any two periods of incapacity for work which are separated by a period of not more than 8 weeks shall be treated as a single period of incapacity for work.
@ref s 152(3) (src:3113)
GIVEN `the last day of the earlier period of incapacity for work` IS A DATE
      `the first day of the later period of incapacity for work` IS A DATE
      `the number of weeks specified in subsection (3)` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 152(3) the two periods of incapacity for work are treated as a single period` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the first day of the later period of incapacity for work` AT MOST `the last day of the earlier period of incapacity for work`
            THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): periods that overlap are not "separated by a period"
        IF ((`the first day of the later period of incapacity for work` MINUS `the last day of the earlier period of incapacity for work`) MINUS 1)
               AT MOST (`the number of weeks specified in subsection (3)` TIMES 7)
            THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "separated by a period of not more than 8 weeks"

-- Plain gloss: a day can be a day of incapacity for work even though it is before the contract
-- was made or after it ended, or is not a day the contract required the employee to be available.
-- src:3116 | (6) A day may be a day of incapacity for work in relation to a contract of service, and so form part of a period of incapacity for work, notwithstanding that—
-- src:3117 | (a) it falls before the making of the contract or after the contract expires or is brought to an end; or
-- src:3118 | (b) it is not a day on which the employee concerned would be required by that contract to be available for work.
@ref s 152(6) (src:3116-3118)
GIVEN `the day falls before the making of the contract or after the contract expires or is brought to an end` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day is not a day on which the employee would be required by the contract to be available for work` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 152(6) the day may be a day of incapacity for work notwithstanding` MEANS
    IF     `the day falls before the making of the contract or after the contract expires or is brought to an end`
        OR `the day is not a day on which the employee would be required by the contract to be available for work`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "may be a day of incapacity ... notwithstanding that" names two cases only to say they do not prevent it

§§ `s 153 Period of entitlement`

-- Plain gloss: a period of entitlement runs from the first day of a period of incapacity for work
-- to the first of four ends; if it has not yet ended its last day is unknown.
DECLARE `s 153 Period of entitlement` HAS
    `the first day` IS A DATE
    `the last day`  IS A MAYBE DATE

-- Helper (it implements no provision): the earlier of two days that may be missing.
GIVEN a IS A MAYBE DATE
      b IS A MAYBE DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`s 153(2) the earlier of two optional days` MEANS
    CONSIDER a
    WHEN NOTHING THEN b
    WHEN JUST x THEN
        CONSIDER b
        WHEN NOTHING THEN JUST x
        WHEN JUST y THEN IF x AT MOST y THEN JUST x ELSE JUST y

-- Plain gloss: for a woman who is, or has been, pregnant, the period of entitlement also ends the
-- day before her disqualifying period (maternity pay period or maternity allowance period) begins
-- (limb (d)). Limb (d) does not apply if the pregnancy ended before that period began other than
-- by a confinement (subsection (11)).
-- src:3125 | (d) in the case of an employee who is, or has been, pregnant, the day immediately preceding the beginning of the disqualifying period.
-- src:3136 | (11) Subsection (2)(d) above does not apply in relation to an employee who has been pregnant if her pregnancy terminated, before the beginning of the disqualifying period, otherwise than by confinement.
@ref s 153(2)(d), (11) (src:3125) (src:3136)
GIVEN `the employee is, or has been, pregnant` IS A BOOLEAN
      `her pregnancy terminated, before the beginning of the disqualifying period, otherwise than by confinement` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 153(2)(d) the period of entitlement ends on the day before the disqualifying period` MEANS
    IF NOT `the employee is, or has been, pregnant`
    THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "in the case of an employee who is, or has been, pregnant"
    ELSE CONSIDER `her pregnancy terminated, before the beginning of the disqualifying period, otherwise than by confinement`
         WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether the pregnancy ended otherwise than by confinement is s 171(1)'s answer
         WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Subsection (2)(d) above does not apply in relation to an employee who has been pregnant if her pregnancy terminated, before the beginning of the disqualifying period, otherwise than by confinement."
         WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST TRUE

-- Plain gloss: the period of entitlement ends on whichever comes first: the last day of the period
-- of incapacity for work, the day the employee reaches the maximum entitlement, the day the
-- contract ends, or (for a pregnant employee) the day before her disqualifying period. A limb that
-- has not happened has no day. If none has happened the period has not ended, and NOTHING is the answer.
-- src:3121 | (2) For the purposes of this Part of this Act a period of entitlement, as between an employee and his employer, is a period beginning with the commencement of a period of incapacity for work and ending with whichever of the following first occurs—
-- src:3122 | (a) the termination of that period of incapacity for work;
-- src:3123 | (b) the day on which the employee reaches, as against the employer concerned, his maximum entitlement to statutory sick pay (determined in accordance with section 155 below);
-- src:3124 | (c) the day on which the employee’s contract of service with the employer concerned expires or is brought to an end;
-- src:3125 | (d) in the case of an employee who is, or has been, pregnant, the day immediately preceding the beginning of the disqualifying period.
@ref s 153(2) (src:3121-3125)
GIVEN `the last day of the period of incapacity for work` IS A MAYBE DATE
      `the day on which the employee reaches his maximum entitlement to statutory sick pay` IS A MAYBE DATE
      `the day on which the contract of service expires or is brought to an end` IS A MAYBE DATE
      `the day immediately preceding the beginning of the disqualifying period` IS A MAYBE DATE
      `limb (d) applies` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`s 153(2) the period of entitlement ends on` MEANS
    CONSIDER `limb (d) applies`
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN `s 153(2) the earlier of two optional days` allButD `the day immediately preceding the beginning of the disqualifying period`
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN allButD
    WHEN NOTHING THEN
        CONSIDER `the day immediately preceding the beginning of the disqualifying period`
        WHEN NOTHING THEN allButD
        WHEN JUST d THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether limb (d) applies is s 153(2)(d) and (11)'s answer
    WHERE
        allButD MEANS
            `s 153(2) the earlier of two optional days`
                `the last day of the period of incapacity for work`
                (`s 153(2) the earlier of two optional days`
                    `the day on which the employee reaches his maximum entitlement to statutory sick pay`
                    `the day on which the contract of service expires or is brought to an end`)

-- Plain gloss: the day the period of entitlement begins. Normally the first day of the period of
-- incapacity for work (subsection (2)). But if the contract first takes effect on a day within a
-- period of incapacity for work, the period begins that day (7); if it first takes effect between two
-- periods treated as one by s 152(3), the period begins with the first day of the second (8);
-- and if the earnings for the day it takes effect do not attract secondary Class 1 liability, the day
-- that counts is the first day on which they do (9).
-- src:3132 | (7) In a case where the employee’s contract of service first takes effect on a day which falls within a period of incapacity for work, the period of entitlement begins with that day.
-- src:3133 | (8) In a case where the employee’s contract of service first takes effect between two periods of incapacity for work which by virtue of section 152(3) above are treated as one, the period of entitlement begins with the first day of the second of those periods.
-- src:3134 | (9) In any case where, otherwise than by virtue of section 6(1)(b) above, an employee’s earnings under a contract of service in respect of the day on which the contract takes effect do not attract a liability to pay secondary Class 1 contributions, subsections (7) and (8) above shall have effect as if for any reference to the contract first taking effect there were substituted a reference to the first day in respect of which the employee’s earnings attract such a liability.
@ref s 153(7), (8), (9) (src:3132-3134)
GIVEN `the first day of the period of incapacity for work` IS A DATE
      `the day the contract of service first takes effect` IS A DATE
      `subsection (9) applies: the earnings for that day do not attract a liability to pay secondary Class 1 contributions` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the first day in respect of which the employee's earnings attract such a liability` IS A DATE
      `that day falls within a period of incapacity for work` IS A BOOLEAN
      `that day falls between two periods of incapacity for work treated as one by section 152(3)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the first day of the second of those periods` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`s 153(7) the period of entitlement begins on` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `that day falls within a period of incapacity for work`
            THEN theDay
        IF `that day falls between two periods of incapacity for work treated as one by section 152(3)`
            THEN `the first day of the second of those periods`
        OTHERWISE `the first day of the period of incapacity for work`
    WHERE
        theDay MEANS
            IF `subsection (9) applies: the earnings for that day do not attract a liability to pay secondary Class 1 contributions`
            THEN `the first day in respect of which the employee's earnings attract such a liability`
            ELSE `the day the contract of service first takes effect`

-- Plain gloss: the first condition is that the day falls within a period of entitlement.
-- Schedule 11 (another part's range) lists circumstances in which no period of entitlement arises;
-- its answer is an input. A period that has not ended has no last day.
-- src:3120 | (1) The first condition is that the day in question falls within a period which is, as between the employee and his employer, a period of entitlement.
-- src:3126 | (3) Schedule 11 to this Act has effect for the purpose of specifying circumstances in which a period of entitlement does not arise in relation to a particular period of incapacity for work.
@ref s 153(1), (3) (src:3120) (src:3126)
GIVEN day IS A DATE
      period IS A `s 153 Period of entitlement`
      `sch 11 a circumstance specified in Schedule 11 prevents a period of entitlement arising in relation to the period of incapacity for work` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 153(1) the first condition is satisfied` MEANS
    CONSIDER `sch 11 a circumstance specified in Schedule 11 prevents a period of entitlement arising in relation to the period of incapacity for work`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): Schedule 11 says when a period of entitlement does not arise
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Schedule 11 to this Act has effect for the purpose of specifying circumstances in which a period of entitlement does not arise"
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN
        IF day AT LEAST period's `the first day` AND withinEnd
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The first condition is that the day in question falls within a period which is, as between the employee and his employer, a period of entitlement."
    WHERE
        withinEnd MEANS
            CONSIDER period's `the last day`
            WHEN NOTHING THEN TRUE
            WHEN JUST lastDay THEN day AT MOST lastDay

-- Plain gloss: a period of entitlement as between the employee and one employer may also be a
-- period of entitlement as between him and another employer of his.
-- src:3127 | (4) A period of entitlement as between an employee and an employer of his may also be, or form part of, a period of entitlement as between him and another employer of his.
@ref s 153(4) (src:3127)
GIVEN `the other person is also an employer of the employee` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 153(4) the period is also a period of entitlement as between the employee and another employer` MEANS
    IF `the other person is also an employer of the employee`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "may also be" names a possibility for another employer of his

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- s 153(12): the four terms it defines.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- Plain gloss: "confinement" is read as s 171(1) reads it (ssc6-smp).
-- src:3138 | “confinement” is to be construed in accordance with section 171(1) below;
@ref s 153(12) "confinement" (src:3138)
GIVEN labour IS A Labour
      `the labour resulted in the issue of a child, alive or dead` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 153(12) she has been confined` MEANS
    `s 171(1) she is confined` labour `the labour resulted in the issue of a child, alive or dead`

-- Plain gloss: for a woman entitled to statutory maternity pay the disqualifying period is her
-- maternity pay period; for a woman entitled to maternity allowance it is her maternity allowance
-- period (s 35(2), another part's range: seam S24). The answer ssc9's Schedule 11 asks for (seam S32).
-- A woman entitled to both, or to neither, is a case the definition does not cover.
-- src:3139 | “disqualifying period” means—
-- src:3140 | in relation to a woman entitled to statutory maternity pay, the maternity pay period; and
-- src:3141 | in relation to a woman entitled to maternity allowance, the maternity allowance period;
@ref s 153(12) "disqualifying period" (src:3139-3141)
GIVEN `the relevant date` IS A DATE
      `she is entitled to statutory maternity pay` IS A BOOLEAN
      `she is entitled to maternity allowance` IS A BOOLEAN
      `s 165(1) the maternity pay period` IS A Period
      `s 35(2) the maternity allowance period` IS A Period
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 153(12) the relevant date falls within the disqualifying period` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `she is entitled to statutory maternity pay` AND `she is entitled to maternity allowance`
            THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the definition gives a period "in relation to a woman entitled to statutory maternity pay" and another "in relation to a woman entitled to maternity allowance", and does not say which prevails for a woman entitled to both
        IF `she is entitled to statutory maternity pay`
            THEN within `s 165(1) the maternity pay period`
        IF `she is entitled to maternity allowance`
            THEN within `s 35(2) the maternity allowance period`
        OTHERWISE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "disqualifying period" is defined only "in relation to a woman entitled to statutory maternity pay" or "to maternity allowance"
    WHERE
        within p MEANS
            IF `the relevant date` AT LEAST p's `the first day` AND `the relevant date` AT MOST p's `the last day`
            THEN JUST TRUE
            ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“disqualifying period” means— in relation to a woman entitled to statutory maternity pay, the maternity pay period; and in relation to a woman entitled to maternity allowance, the maternity allowance period"

-- Plain gloss: "maternity allowance period" has the meaning s 35(2) gives it (another part's
-- range): the answer is that part's, passed through.
-- src:3142 | “maternity allowance period” has the meaning assigned to it by section 35(2) above, and
@ref s 153(12) "maternity allowance period" (src:3142)
GIVEN `s 35(2) the maternity allowance period` IS A Period
GIVETH A Period
`s 153(12) the maternity allowance period` MEANS `s 35(2) the maternity allowance period`

-- Plain gloss: "maternity pay period" has the meaning s 165(1) gives it (ssc6-smp).
-- src:3143 | “maternity pay period” has the meaning assigned to it by section 165(1) below.
@ref s 153(12) "maternity pay period" (src:3143)
GIVEN `the first day of the maternity pay period` IS A DATE
      `the duration of the prescribed period in weeks` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE Period
`s 153(12) the maternity pay period` MEANS
    `s 165(1) the maternity pay period` `the first day of the maternity pay period` `the duration of the prescribed period in weeks`

§§ `s 154 Qualifying days`

-- Plain gloss: the qualifying days are the days the employee and employer agree (days he must be
-- available to work, or chosen to reflect the contract); if they do not agree, the days regulations
-- determine. Regulations determining them are outside the sources, so that answer is an input.
-- src:3146 | (2) The days which are for the purposes of this Part of this Act to be qualifying days as between an employee and an employer of his (that is to say, those days of the week on which he is required by his contract of service with that employer to be available for work or which are chosen to reflect the terms of that contract) shall be such day or days as may, subject to regulations, be agreed between the employee and his employer or, failing such agreement, determined in accordance with regulations.
@ref s 154(2) (src:3146)
GIVEN `the employee and his employer have agreed which days are qualifying days` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day is one of the days agreed` IS A BOOLEAN
      `regulations determine the day to be a qualifying day` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 154(2) the day is a qualifying day` MEANS
    IF `the employee and his employer have agreed which days are qualifying days`
    THEN
        IF `the day is one of the days agreed`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "shall be such day or days as may, subject to regulations, be agreed between the employee and his employer"
    ELSE `regulations determine the day to be a qualifying day`        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text) when the caller does not supply the regulations' answer

-- Plain gloss: the second condition is that the day in question is a qualifying day.
-- src:3145 | (1) The second condition is that the day in question is a qualifying day.
@ref s 154(1) (src:3145)
GIVEN `s 154(2) the day is a qualifying day` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 154(1) the second condition is satisfied` MEANS
    `s 154(2) the day is a qualifying day`        -- JUST FALSE here is s 154(2)'s "no", whose comment quotes the text

-- Plain gloss: if the days are fixed by agreement, every week (a week begins on Sunday) must
-- contain at least one qualifying day.
-- src:3147 | (3) In any case where qualifying days are determined by agreement between an employee and his employer there shall, in each week (beginning with Sunday), be at least one qualifying day.
@ref s 154(3) (src:3147)
GIVEN `the number of qualifying days in the week, beginning with Sunday, under the agreement` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 154(3) the agreement provides at least one qualifying day in the week` MEANS
    IF `the number of qualifying days in the week, beginning with Sunday, under the agreement` AT LEAST 1
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "there shall, in each week (beginning with Sunday), be at least one qualifying day" (marginal reading: a "shall" requirement is taken as the text's no)

-- Plain gloss: a qualifying day as between the employee and one employer may also be a
-- qualifying day as between him and another employer of his.
-- src:3148 | (4) A day which is a qualifying day as between an employee and an employer of his may also be a qualifying day as between him and another employer of his.
@ref s 154(4) (src:3148)
GIVEN `the other person is also an employer of the employee` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 154(4) the day is also a qualifying day as between the employee and another employer` MEANS
    IF `the other person is also an employer of the employee`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "may also be" names a possibility for another employer of his

§§ `s 155 Limitations on entitlement`

-- The Act's own figure in subsection (4).
-- src:3154 | (4) The entitlement limit is an amount equal to 28 times the weekly rate applicable in accordance with section 157 below.
@ref s 155(4) (src:3154)
`s 155(4) the number of times the weekly rate that makes up the entitlement limit` MEANS 28

-- Plain gloss: the most statutory sick pay one employer has to pay in a period of entitlement is
-- 28 times the weekly rate (the rate that applies under s 157).
@ref s 155(4) (src:3154)
GIVEN `the weekly rate applicable in accordance with section 157, in pounds` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 155(4) the entitlement limit in pounds` MEANS
    `s 155(4) the number of times the weekly rate that makes up the entitlement limit` TIMES `the weekly rate applicable in accordance with section 157, in pounds`

-- Plain gloss: the maximum entitlement is reached on the day on which the statutory sick pay the
-- employee has become entitled to in the period of entitlement first reaches or passes the limit.
-- The amounts are the daily amounts, in order, from the first day of the period of entitlement.
-- src:3153 | (3) The maximum entitlement as against any one employer is reached on the day on which the amount to which the employee has become entitled by way of statutory sick pay during the period of entitlement in question first reaches or passes the entitlement limit.
@ref s 155(3) (src:3153)
GIVEN limit IS A NUMBER
      `the amount to which the employee had become entitled before the first of these days` IS A NUMBER
      `the first of these days` IS A DATE
      `the amounts of statutory sick pay for these days, in order, in pounds` IS A LIST OF NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`s 155(3) the day on which the maximum entitlement is reached` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the amounts of statutory sick pay for these days, in order, in pounds`
    WHEN EMPTY THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the days given do not reach the limit, and the text gives no day for a limit not yet reached
    WHEN amount FOLLOWED BY rest THEN
        IF `the amount to which the employee had become entitled before the first of these days` PLUS amount AT LEAST limit
        THEN JUST `the first of these days`
        ELSE `s 155(3) the day on which the maximum entitlement is reached` limit (`the amount to which the employee had become entitled before the first of these days` PLUS amount) (`the first of these days` PLUS 1) rest

-- Plain gloss: an employee cannot be entitled, as against one employer, to more statutory sick pay
-- in one period of entitlement than the maximum entitlement (the entitlement limit).
-- src:3152 | (2) An employee shall not be entitled, as against any one employer, to an aggregate amount of statutory sick pay in respect of any one period of entitlement which exceeds his maximum entitlement.
@ref s 155(2) (src:3152)
GIVEN `the aggregate amount of statutory sick pay in the period of entitlement, in pounds` IS A NUMBER
      `his maximum entitlement, in pounds` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 155(2) the employee is entitled as against the employer to the aggregate amount` MEANS
    IF `the aggregate amount of statutory sick pay in the period of entitlement, in pounds` GREATER THAN `his maximum entitlement, in pounds`
    THEN JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "An employee shall not be entitled, as against any one employer, to an aggregate amount of statutory sick pay in respect of any one period of entitlement which exceeds his maximum entitlement."
    ELSE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the subsection bars the excess and does not itself grant the rest

§§ `s 156 Notification of incapacity for work`

-- Plain gloss: if the employer has not been duly notified (as regulations require) of the day, the
-- employer may withhold the statutory sick pay for that qualifying day. The words "..." after
-- paragraph (a) and the blank paragraph (b) (src:3159-3160) are repealed text; what is left is encoded.
-- src:3158 | (2) An employer who would, apart from this section, be liable to pay an amount of statutory sick pay to an employee in respect of a qualifying day (the “day in question”) shall be entitled to withhold payment of that amount if—
-- src:3159 | (a) the day in question is one in respect of which he has not been duly notified in accordance with regulations under subsection (1) above; ...
@ref s 156(2) (src:3158-3159)
GIVEN `the employer would, apart from this section, be liable to pay statutory sick pay in respect of the qualifying day` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the employer has not been duly notified in accordance with regulations under subsection (1) of the day in question` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 156(2) the employer is entitled to withhold payment for the day` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the employer would, apart from this section, be liable to pay statutory sick pay in respect of the qualifying day`
            THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "An employer who would, apart from this section, be liable to pay"
        IF `the employer has not been duly notified in accordance with regulations under subsection (1) of the day in question`
            THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "shall be entitled to withhold payment of that amount if" (marginal reading (a): a failed "if" counts as the text's no)

-- Plain gloss: withholding does not shorten the period of entitlement.
-- src:3161 | (3) Where an employer withholds any amount of statutory sick pay under this section—
-- src:3162 | (a) the period of entitlement in question shall not be affected; and
@ref s 156(3)(a) (src:3161-3162)
GIVEN `the employer withholds an amount of statutory sick pay under section 156` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 156(3)(a) the period of entitlement is affected by the withholding` MEANS
    IF `the employer withholds an amount of statutory sick pay under section 156`
    THEN JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "the period of entitlement in question shall not be affected"
    ELSE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the paragraph speaks only of a case where the employer withholds

-- Plain gloss: for working out whether the maximum entitlement has been reached, an amount the
-- employer withholds is not counted as one the employee has become entitled to.
-- src:3161 | (3) Where an employer withholds any amount of statutory sick pay under this section—
-- src:3162 | (a) the period of entitlement in question shall not be affected; and
-- src:3163 | (b) for the purposes of calculating his maximum entitlement in accordance with section 155 above the employee shall not be taken to have become entitled to the amount so withheld.
@ref s 156(3)(b) (src:3161-3163)
GIVEN `the amount of statutory sick pay for the day, in pounds` IS A NUMBER
      `the employer withholds that amount under section 156` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 156(3)(b) the amount counted towards the maximum entitlement in pounds` MEANS
    IF `the employer withholds that amount under section 156`
    THEN 0        -- "the employee shall not be taken to have become entitled to the amount so withheld"
    ELSE `the amount of statutory sick pay for the day, in pounds`

§§ `s 157 Rates of payment`

-- The Act's own figures in subsection (1): £123.25 and 80 per cent. An order under subsection (2) may substitute others.
-- src:3166 | (1) The weekly rate of statutory sick pay that an employer must pay to an employee is the lower of—
-- src:3167 | (a) £123.25, and
-- src:3168 | (b) 80% of the employee’s normal weekly earnings.
@ref s 157(1) (src:3166-3168)
`s 157(1)(a) the weekly rate cap in pounds` MEANS 123.25

`s 157(1)(b) the percentage of normal weekly earnings` MEANS 80%

-- Plain gloss: the weekly rate is the lower of £123.25 and 80 per cent of the employee's normal weekly earnings.
@ref s 157(1) (src:3166-3168)
GIVEN `the employee's normal weekly earnings in pounds` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 157(1) the weekly rate of statutory sick pay in pounds` MEANS
    min `s 157(1)(a) the weekly rate cap in pounds` (`the employee's normal weekly earnings in pounds` TIMES `s 157(1)(b) the percentage of normal weekly earnings`)

-- The figure ss 166(3) and 171ZN(2G) compare a prescribed rate with: "the weekly rate of statutory
-- sick pay for the time being specified in section 157(1)". The Act specifies the £123.25 rate and,
-- as the other, 80 per cent of earnings, which is a proportion and not a specified rate (fork F-specified).
@ref s 157(1)(a) (src:3166-3167)
`s 157(1) the weekly rate of statutory sick pay specified in section 157(1) in pounds` MEANS `s 157(1)(a) the weekly rate cap in pounds`

-- Plain gloss: the amount for one day is the weekly rate that applies that day divided by the number
-- of qualifying days, as between that employer and that employee, in the week (beginning with
-- Sunday) in which the day falls.
-- src:3172 | (3) The amount of statutory sick pay payable by any one employer in respect of any day shall be the weekly rate applicable on that day divided by the number of days which are, in the week (beginning with Sunday) in which that day falls, qualifying days as between that employer and the employee concerned.
@ref s 157(3) (src:3172)
GIVEN `the weekly rate applicable on the day, in pounds` IS A NUMBER
      `the number of qualifying days in the week, beginning with Sunday, in which the day falls` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 157(3) the amount of statutory sick pay payable in respect of the day in pounds` MEANS
    IF `the number of qualifying days in the week, beginning with Sunday, in which the day falls` AT LEAST 1
    THEN JUST (`the weekly rate applicable on the day, in pounds` DIVIDED BY `the number of qualifying days in the week, beginning with Sunday, in which the day falls`)
    ELSE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): a week with no qualifying day has no number to divide by

§§ `s 159A Power to provide for recovery by employers`

-- Plain gloss: "contributions payments" are payments a person is required by or under an enactment to
-- make in discharge of a liability as an employer for primary or secondary Class 1 contributions.
-- src:3186 | “contributions payments” means payments which a person is required by or under any enactment to make in discharge of any liability of his as an employer in respect of primary or secondary Class 1 contributions; and
@ref s 159A(3) "contributions payments" (src:3186)
GIVEN `the payment is one which a person is required by or under any enactment to make` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is made in discharge of a liability of his as an employer in respect of primary or secondary Class 1 contributions` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 159A(3) the payment is a contributions payment` MEANS
    IF     `the payment is one which a person is required by or under any enactment to make`
       AND `it is made in discharge of a liability of his as an employer in respect of primary or secondary Class 1 contributions`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“contributions payments” means payments which a person is required by or under any enactment to make in discharge of any liability of his as an employer in respect of primary or secondary Class 1 contributions"

-- Plain gloss: a percentage or minimum is "specified" if an order under subsection (1) specifies it or
-- it is determined in accordance with such an order.
-- src:3187 | “specified” means specified in or determined in accordance with an order under subsection (1).
@ref s 159A(3) "specified" (src:3187)
GIVEN `it is specified in or determined in accordance with an order under subsection (1)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 159A(3) the figure is specified` MEANS
    IF `it is specified in or determined in accordance with an order under subsection (1)`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“specified” means specified in or determined in accordance with an order under subsection (1)"

§§ `s 160 Relationship with benefits and other payments`

-- Plain gloss: Schedule 12 says how statutory sick pay interacts with other benefits and payments.
-- The Schedule is in another part's range, so its answer is an input (a seam found by ssc6).
-- src:3192 | 160 Relationship with benefits and other payments, etc.
-- src:3193 | Schedule 12 to this Act has effect with respect to the relationship between statutory sick pay and certain benefits and payments.
@ref s 160 (src:3192-3193)
GIVEN `sch 12 the relationship between statutory sick pay and the benefit or payment is as Schedule 12 provides` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 160 Schedule 12 has effect with respect to the relationship between statutory sick pay and the benefit or payment` MEANS
    `sch 12 the relationship between statutory sick pay and the benefit or payment is as Schedule 12 provides`        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text) when Schedule 12 is not consulted

§§ `s 161 Crown employment`

-- Plain gloss: Part XI covers people employed by or under the Crown as it covers others, except
-- members of Her Majesty's forces in that capacity.
-- src:3194 | 161 Crown employment - Part XI.
-- src:3195 | (1) Subject to subsection (2) below, the provisions of this Part of this Act apply in relation to persons employed by or under the Crown as they apply in relation to persons employed otherwise than by or under the Crown.
-- src:3196 | (2) The provisions of this Part of this Act do not apply in relation to persons serving as members of Her Majesty’s forces, in their capacity as such.
@ref s 161(1), (2) (src:3194-3196)
GIVEN `the person is employed by or under the Crown` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is serving as a member of Her Majesty's forces, in that capacity` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 161 Part XI applies to the person employed by or under the Crown` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the person is serving as a member of Her Majesty's forces, in that capacity`
            THEN JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "the provisions of this Part of this Act do not apply in relation to persons serving as members of Her Majesty's forces, in their capacity as such"
        IF `the person is employed by or under the Crown`
            THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the section speaks only of persons employed by or under the Crown

-- Plain gloss: Her Majesty's forces are the establishments and organisations regulations prescribe
-- (with the Treasury's concurrence), being ones in which persons serve under the Defence Council.
-- src:3197 | (3) For the purposes of this section Her Majesty’s forces shall be taken to consist of such establishments and organisations as may be prescribed by regulations made by the Secretary of State with the concurrence of the Treasury, being establishments and organisations in which persons serve under the control of the Defence Council.
@ref s 161(3) (src:3197)
GIVEN `the establishment or organisation is prescribed by regulations made by the Secretary of State with the concurrence of the Treasury` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `persons serve in it under the control of the Defence Council` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 161(3) the establishment or organisation is part of Her Majesty's forces` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the establishment or organisation is prescribed by regulations made by the Secretary of State with the concurrence of the Treasury`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the establishments are those "as may be prescribed"
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "shall be taken to consist of such establishments and organisations as may be prescribed"
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN
        IF `persons serve in it under the control of the Defence Council`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "being establishments and organisations in which persons serve under the control of the Defence Council"

§§ `s 162 Special classes of persons`

-- Plain gloss: regulations can change Part XI for a person employed on a ship, vessel, hovercraft
-- or aircraft, or outside Great Britain at a prescribed time, or in prescribed employment on the
-- continental shelf. The power is machinery (the coverage table says so); what it means to the
-- person is whether they are in a class it can reach. Seam S25 supplies the s 120(2) definition.
-- src:3199 | (1) The Secretary of State may with the concurrence of the Treasury make regulations modifying this Part of this Act in such manner as he thinks proper in their application to any person who is, has been or is to be—
-- src:3200 | (a) employed on board any ship, vessel, hovercraft or aircraft;
-- src:3201 | (b) outside Great Britain at any prescribed time or in any prescribed circumstances; or
-- src:3202 | (c) in prescribed employment in connection with continental shelf operations, as defined in section 120(2) above.
@ref s 162(1) (src:3199-3202)
GIVEN `the person is employed on board a ship, vessel, hovercraft or aircraft` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is outside Great Britain at a prescribed time or in prescribed circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN
      `s 120(2) the employment is in connection with continental shelf operations` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the employment is prescribed employment` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 162(1) regulations may modify Part XI in its application to the person` MEANS
    IF     `the person is employed on board a ship, vessel, hovercraft or aircraft`
        OR `the person is outside Great Britain at a prescribed time or in prescribed circumstances`
        OR (`the employment is prescribed employment` AND `s 120(2) the employment is in connection with continental shelf operations`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the power reaches persons "employed on board any ship, vessel, hovercraft or aircraft", "outside Great Britain" or in "prescribed employment in connection with continental shelf operations"

§§ `s 163 Interpretation of Part XI and supplementary provisions`

-- Plain gloss: "contract of service" includes any arrangement providing the terms of appointment of
-- an employee, except where the phrase is used in paragraph (a) of the definition of "employee".
-- The word "includes" leaves the ordinary meaning open, so a case outside it is NOTHING.
-- src:3210 | “contract of service” (except in paragraph (a) of the definition below of “employee”) includes any arrangement providing for the terms of appointment of an employee;
@ref s 163(1) "contract of service" (src:3210)
GIVEN `it is a contract of service in the ordinary sense` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is an arrangement providing for the terms of appointment of an employee` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the phrase is used in paragraph (a) of the definition of employee` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 163(1) the arrangement counts as a contract of service` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `it is a contract of service in the ordinary sense`
            THEN JUST TRUE
        IF `it is an arrangement providing for the terms of appointment of an employee` AND NOT `the phrase is used in paragraph (a) of the definition of employee`
            THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (includes): "“contract of service” (except in paragraph (a) of the definition below of “employee”) includes any arrangement"

-- Plain gloss: an "employee" is a person gainfully employed in Great Britain, under a contract of
-- service or in an office, with earnings (as Parts 1 to 5 use that word); but regulations can exclude
-- some such persons and include some others. The converter shows "..." and a line of dots
-- (src:3212-3213): words and a limb repealed. Whether work on the sea adjacent to Great Britain is
-- "in Great Britain" is s 172(a)'s answer, asked as an input.
-- src:3211 | “employee” means a person who is—
-- src:3212 | gainfully employed in Great Britain either under a contract of service or in an office (including elective office) with earnings (within the meaning of Parts 1 to 5 above); ...
-- src:3214 | but subject to regulations, which may provide for cases where any such person is not to be treated as an employee for the purposes of this Part of this Act and for cases where any person who would not otherwise be an employee for those purposes is to be treated as an employee for those purposes;
@ref s 163(1) "employee" (src:3211-3214)
GIVEN employment IS AN Employment
      `the person is gainfully employed` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the work is carried on in the territorial waters adjacent to Great Britain` IS A BOOLEAN
      `s 172(a) a reference to Great Britain includes the place` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the employment is with earnings within the meaning of Parts 1 to 5` IS A BOOLEAN
      `regulations provide that the person is not to be treated as an employee` IS A BOOLEAN
      `regulations provide that the person is to be treated as an employee` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 163(1) the person is an employee` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `regulations provide that the person is not to be treated as an employee` AND `regulations provide that the person is to be treated as an employee`
            THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the two kinds of regulation cannot both apply to one person, and the text does not say which prevails
        IF `regulations provide that the person is to be treated as an employee`
            THEN JUST TRUE
        IF `regulations provide that the person is not to be treated as an employee`
            THEN JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "cases where any such person is not to be treated as an employee for the purposes of this Part of this Act"
        OTHERWISE definition
    WHERE
        inGreatBritain MEANS
            IF employment's `carried on in Great Britain`
            THEN JUST TRUE
            ELSE IF `the work is carried on in the territorial waters adjacent to Great Britain`
                 THEN `s 172(a) a reference to Great Britain includes the place`
                 ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“employee” means a person who is gainfully employed in Great Britain" (work outside Great Britain and outside the adjacent waters)
        restOfDefinition MEANS
                `the person is gainfully employed`
            AND (employment's `held under a contract of service` OR employment's `held in an office`)
            AND `the employment is with earnings within the meaning of Parts 1 to 5`
        definition MEANS
            CONSIDER inGreatBritain
            WHEN NOTHING THEN
                IF restOfDefinition
                THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether the adjacent waters are in Great Britain is s 172(a)'s answer
                ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“employee” means a person who is gainfully employed in Great Britain" (a limb other than the place already fails)
            WHEN JUST inside THEN
                IF inside AND restOfDefinition
                THEN JUST TRUE
                ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“employee” means a person who is gainfully employed in Great Britain either under a contract of service or in an office (including elective office) with earnings"

-- Plain gloss: the "employer" of an employee under a contract is the person who, under s 6, has to
-- pay secondary Class 1 contributions on the earnings under it, or who would have to but for the
-- s 6(1)(b) condition or because the employee is under 16. Whether s 6 makes someone liable is
-- ssc1's answer, asked as an input.
-- src:3215 | “employer”, in relation to an employee and a contract of service of his, means a person who—
-- src:3216 | under section 6 above is liable to pay secondary Class 1 contributions in relation to any earnings of the employee under the contract, or
-- src:3217 | would be liable to pay such contributions but for—
-- src:3218 | the condition in section 6(1)(b), or
-- src:3219 | the employee being under the age of 16;
@ref s 163(1) "employer" (src:3215-3219)
GIVEN `s 6 the person is liable to pay secondary Class 1 contributions in relation to any earnings of the employee under the contract` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the person would be liable to pay such contributions but for the condition in section 6(1)(b)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person would be liable to pay such contributions but for the employee being under the age of 16` IS A BOOLEAN
      `s 173(a) the person is under 16` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 163(1) the person is the employer in relation to the employee and the contract of service` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `s 6 the person is liable to pay secondary Class 1 contributions in relation to any earnings of the employee under the contract` EQUALS JUST TRUE
            THEN JUST TRUE
        IF `the person would be liable to pay such contributions but for the condition in section 6(1)(b)`
            THEN JUST TRUE
        IF `the person would be liable to pay such contributions but for the employee being under the age of 16` AND `s 173(a) the person is under 16` EQUALS JUST TRUE
            THEN JUST TRUE
        IF `s 6 the person is liable to pay secondary Class 1 contributions in relation to any earnings of the employee under the contract` EQUALS NOTHING
            THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether s 6 makes the person liable is ssc1's answer
        IF `the person would be liable to pay such contributions but for the employee being under the age of 16` AND `s 173(a) the person is under 16` EQUALS NOTHING
            THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether the employee is under 16 is s 173(a)'s answer
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“employer”, in relation to an employee and a contract of service of his, means a person who"

-- Plain gloss: three terms are defined by pointing at the section that gives their meaning.
DECLARE `s 163(1) Term defined by pointing` IS ONE OF
    `s 163(1) period of entitlement`
    `s 163(1) period of incapacity for work`
    `s 163(1) qualifying day`

-- src:3220 | “period of entitlement” has the meaning given by section 153 above;
-- src:3221 | “period of incapacity for work” has the meaning given by section 152 above;
-- src:3224 | “qualifying day” has the meaning given by section 154 above;
@ref s 163(1) "period of entitlement", "period of incapacity for work", "qualifying day" (src:3220-3221) (src:3224)
GIVEN term IS A `s 163(1) Term defined by pointing`
GIVETH A STRING
`s 163(1) the section that gives the meaning of the term` MEANS
    CONSIDER term
    WHEN `s 163(1) period of entitlement` THEN "section 153"
    WHEN `s 163(1) period of incapacity for work` THEN "section 152"
    WHEN `s 163(1) qualifying day` THEN "section 154"

-- Plain gloss: in Part XI "prescribed" means prescribed by regulations.
-- src:3223 | “prescribed” means prescribed by regulations;
@ref s 163(1) "prescribed" (src:3223)
GIVEN `it is prescribed by regulations` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 163(1) the thing is prescribed` MEANS
    IF `it is prescribed by regulations`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“prescribed” means prescribed by regulations"

-- Plain gloss: in Part XI "week" means any period of 7 days.
-- src:3225 | “week” means any period of 7 days.
@ref s 163(1) "week" (src:3225)
`s 163(1) the week convention of Part XI` MEANS `any period of 7 days`

-- Plain gloss: the employee's normal weekly earnings are the average weekly earnings paid to him,
-- or for his benefit, under his contract of service with the employer in question during the
-- "relevant period" (subsections (2) and (3)). "Earnings" and "relevant period" have the meanings
-- regulations give them (so the caller supplies the earnings and the number of weeks), and in
-- prescribed cases regulations calculate them instead (subsection (4)).
-- src:3226 | (2) For the purposes of this Part of this Act an employee’s normal weekly earnings shall, subject to subsection (4) below, be taken to be the average weekly earnings which in the relevant period have been paid to him or paid for his benefit under his contract of service with the employer in question.
-- src:3227 | (3) For the purposes of subsection (2) above, the expressions “earnings” and “relevant period” shall have the meaning given to them by regulations.
-- src:3228 | (4) In such cases as may be prescribed an employee’s normal weekly earnings shall be calculated in accordance with regulations.
@ref s 163(2), (3), (4) (src:3226-3228)
GIVEN `the earnings paid to him or for his benefit under his contract of service with the employer in question in the relevant period, in pounds` IS A NUMBER
      `the number of weeks in the relevant period` IS A NUMBER
      `the case is one in which regulations require his normal weekly earnings to be calculated in accordance with regulations` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 163(2) the employee's normal weekly earnings in pounds` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the case is one in which regulations require his normal weekly earnings to be calculated in accordance with regulations`
            THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "In such cases as may be prescribed an employee's normal weekly earnings shall be calculated in accordance with regulations"
        IF `the number of weeks in the relevant period` AT MOST 0
            THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): "relevant period" has the meaning regulations give it; a period of no weeks has no average
        OTHERWISE JUST (`the earnings paid to him or for his benefit under his contract of service with the employer in question in the relevant period, in pounds` DIVIDED BY `the number of weeks in the relevant period`)
